<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_voltage-batteries" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Ideal Batteries and Wires</title>

	<corollary xml:id="vid-voltage">
	<title>Voltage</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="zpsPTBHeFT4"><description>Introduction to voltage.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<p>Essential to analyzing circuits is to have a model for the <alert>voltage</alert> across each possible circuit element, starting with ideal batteries and ideal wires.</p>

	<definition xml:id="def_battery">
	<title>Battery</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>A <term>battery</term> is a circuit element that provides a source of voltage.</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					<alert>There is a voltage difference between the terminals of a battery.</alert>  The <m>+</m> terminal of the battery is at a higher voltage than the <m>-</m> terminal.<!--  This voltage difference is sometimes known as an <em>electromotive force</em> (emf).-->
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					<alert>A battery stores chemical potential energy.</alert>  When the battery is part of a complete circuit, the chemical energy is converted to electric potential energy by separating charged ions in a chemical solution.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					<alert>The separated ions create an electric field.</alert>  When the battery is part of a complete circuit, an electric field exists in both the battery and in the conductors that make up the circuit.  These conductors are thus <alert>not in electrostatic equilibrium</alert>.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<figure xml:id="BatteryInside">
			  <title>Battery Diagram</title>
			<caption><alert>Example:</alert> simplified charge diagram and electric field vectors for a battery (left) connected in a complete circuit to a conducting wire (right).  The charges in the battery represent ions that have moved as a result of a chemical reaction occurring inside the battery; the charges on the wire result from electrons moving in response to the voltage provided by the battery.  The resulting electric field inside the conducting wire forces current to move.</caption>
			<image source="BatteryInside.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a stylized representation of a battery on the left and a conducting wire on the right.  The battery has positive charges along its top and negative charges along its bottom, with downward vectors representing the electric field inside.  The conducting wire has charges along its side, with downward vectors representing the electric field inside.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</statement>
	</definition>

	<assumption xml:id="a_battery">
	<title>Ideal Battery</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>An <term>ideal battery</term> has a constant voltage that does not change in time and is not dependent on what the battery is connected to.  The voltage is higher at the positive end of the battery.</p>
	</statement>
	<figure xml:id="CD_Battery">
			  <title>Battery Diagram</title>
			<caption>Two representations of a battery: a picture on the left and a circuit diagram on the right.</caption>
			<image source="CD_Battery.png" width = "50%" ><description>Two representations of a battery: a picture of a battery on the left and a circuit diagram with a long line parallel to a short line on the right.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</assumption>
	
	<assumption xml:id="a_wire">
	<title>Ideal Wire</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>An <term>ideal wire</term> has no effective resistance to the flow of current, and the voltage across it is always equal to zero.  Metal objects that are good electrical conductors (such as copper) make good ideal wires.</p>
	</statement>
	<figure xml:id="CD_Wire">
			  <title>Wire Diagram</title>
			<caption>Two representations of a wire: a picture on the left and a circuit diagram on the right.</caption>
			<image source="CD_Wire.png" width = "50%" ><description>Two representations of a wire: a red laboratory wire on the left and a straight line on the right.</description></image>
		</figure>
</assumption>

<subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	<activity>
		<title>Properties of Ideal Batteries and Wires</title>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Which of the following statements are true?
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					When the battery is in a complete circuit, it is in electrostatic equilibrium
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The positive terminal of the battery is at a higher voltage than the negative terminal of the battery
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The negative terminal of the battery always has a voltage equal to zero
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The voltage across an ideal wire is equal to zero
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</activity>
	
	</subsubsection>

		
		

